<!-- ================= HOW TO USE ================= -->
<section>
<h2>How to Use This Poster</h2>
<p>
This poster is designed as a quick training reference for the pass, the
back office, or a staff noticeboard — print it, pin it, or keep it on
a tablet in the kitchen or service area. It gives new team members a fast,
memorable way to tell a sauce from a condiment when a guest or a trainer
asks the difference.
</p>
<div class="card">
<h2 style="border:none;padding-left:0;font-size:1.3rem;">The Easiest Way to Remember</h2>
<p>
A <strong>sauce</strong> complements the dish — it's usually prepared and plated
with the food. A <strong>condiment</strong> customises the dish — the guest
decides how much to use, typically at the table. Some products can be either,
depending on how they're prepared, served and used.
</p>
</div>
</section>
<!-- ================= EXAMPLES ================= -->
<section>
<h2>Common Sauces vs Condiments</h2>
<div class="grid-list">
<div class="item"><strong>Béchamel</strong><span>Sauce</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Ketchup</strong><span>Condiment</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Demi-glace</strong><span>Sauce</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Mustard</strong><span>Condiment</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Tomato Sauce</strong><span>Sauce</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Mayonnaise</strong><span>Condiment</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Hollandaise</strong><span>Sauce</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Pickles</strong><span>Condiment</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Mushroom Sauce</strong><span>Sauce</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Chutneys</strong><span>Condiment</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Pepper Sauce</strong><span>Sauce</span></div>
<div class="item"><strong>Relishes</strong><span>Condiment</span></div>
</div>
</section>
